## File 2: `src/errors/CustomError.js`

```javascript
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * CUSTOM ERROR CLASSES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * WHY custom error classes instead of plain Error objects?
 *   - They carry a `.status` code (HTTP status) so the error handler can
 *     return 404, 400, etc. without guessing.
 *   - They are type-checkable: `err instanceof NotFoundError` is far more
 *     precise than string-matching on `err.message`.
 *   - They keep the "what went wrong" and "how to respond" in one object.
 *
 * Hierarchy:
 *   Error (built-in)
 *     └── AppError (base for all API errors, carries status + isOperational flag)
 *           ├── NotFoundError  → HTTP 404
 *           ├── ValidationError → HTTP 400
 *           └── InternalError  → HTTP 500
 */

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Base class: AppError
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * All custom API errors extend this class.
 *
 * @param {string} message — Human-readable description of the error.
 * @param {number} statusCode — Corresponding HTTP status code (e.g., 404).
 */
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message);

    // Attach the HTTP status so middleware can read it later.
    this.statusCode = statusCode;

    // "Operational" errors are expected failures (bad input, missing resource).
    // We distinguish them from programming bugs (undefined reference, etc.)
    // so we don't leak stack traces to clients for operational errors.
    this.isOperational = true;

    // Capture a clean stack trace at the point of construction.
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NotFoundError — resource not found (HTTP 404)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Thrown when a requested book does not exist in storage.
 * Example: GET /books/999 when only books 1–5 exist.
 */
class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  constructor(resource = 'resource', id) {
    // Format: "Book with id 999 not found"
    const message = `${resource} with id ${id} not found`;
    super(message, 404);
  }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ValidationError — bad client input (HTTP 400)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Thrown when request body or query parameters fail validation.
 * Example: POST /books with a missing title or negative price.
 */
class ValidationError extends AppError {
  constructor(message = 'Validation failed') {
    super(message, 400);
  }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// InternalError — unexpected server-side failure (HTTP 500)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Thrown for programming bugs or unforeseen conditions.
 * These are NOT operational — we log the full stack but return a generic
 * message to the client so we don't leak internal details.
 */
class InternalError extends AppError {
  constructor(message = 'Internal server error') {
    super(message, 500);
  }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Exports — one export per class for clarity; also a combined object.
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
module.exports = { AppError, NotFoundError, ValidationError, InternalError };
```

**Key design decisions:**
- `isOperational` flag lets the error handler decide whether to log the full stack (bugs) or just return a clean response (expected failures).
- Each subclass sets its own default status code, so callers rarely need to pass one explicitly.

